m Asylum  and his doctor is there and his doctor  looks oddly like the Joker and he's  saying thank God we finally found a  medicine that breaks through to you you  know you've been here for ten years ever  since you killed your parents in that  alley Bruce yeah and he looks around and  his chiropractor looks a lot like Bane  and every you know he's so and as he  starts to discover this is all just a  big cage built for him and sets out on  this Odyssey I think you'll see a very  very change DC Universe the big moment  for me was when Alfred felt like he  betrayed him and you're not sure if you  should trust it because Alfred's choice  makes you question everything even more  exactly because if Alfred's that sense  of right and wrong  so if Alfred's making a choice you're  like Bruce what have you done which i  think is a great use of that character  cuz he's such a strong character oh I  love Alfred as a dad alfred is one of my  favorites to write and one of my least  favorites cuz he always gets hurt you  know it's like he's the father who has a  son who does something that's he loves  and admires and and you know but puts  his son in the line of danger a lot and  I just I feel that with my kids when I  see them do anything dangerous that I'm  still proud of them for doing you know  yeah anything ambitious so yeah Alfred  Alfred is close to my heart he's a  perfect redshirt he's always yeah sure  and that's the problem yes so if last  night on earth represents the end of  this tale where would the Batman who  laughs lies that like a side street yeah  well I guess what I'd say is for me I  think the the way I would sell my work  to everybody out there beyond sort of  the PT Barnum pitches which I love doing  myself anyway and and and all of it is  to say that everything I'm working on  sort of centers on the same ideas and  approaches them from different kind of  prismatic angles but it's stuff I'm  really worried about for my kids and for  the future  it's it really is this idea that the  more sort of self-centered we get over  time the more isolated we get the more  difficult it's going to be to solve  these kind of big systemic problems and  there's this constant pull and  temptation on everything on an  individual level from social media to  the way we consume entertainment to the  way we insulate ourselves with beliefs  and views politically that sort of just  support our own - you know you see these  giant tectonic movements in a global  scale with countries are treating from  each other there's a sense of I just  need to worry about me I'm gonna consume  sort of what I want as opposed to kind  of a sense of smallness and collectivism  and humility and so every book I'm  working on sort of approaches that theme  differently you know Justice League does  and last night is very much about people  falling for that you know that that sort  of pitch from Lex Luthor so Batman II  laughs is almost the darkest culmination  an extreme version of that thinking the  Batman laughs as a character is Bruce's  worst nightmare you know he comes from a  place called the Dark multiverse and it  imagines that all of our hopes and fears  exist in this place and he's Bruce's  fear that one day he'll cross the line  kill the Joker in the Joker's heart will  contain a toxin that makes whoever kills  him the next Joker right so it's Batman  completely devoid of morals enough  and he's the Batman who always wins his  whole goal is just to win he's sort of  the apex predator of his world he's  destroyed world after world in this dark  realm and now he comes here to face off  with Batman and so his deep belief is  that Batman the bat is the only mammal  that can fly right and he believes that  the bats job is to show people how to  become the thing they were always meant  to become and soar and that is this  belief that evil and selfishness is  something that isn't found in the  natural world except in humans and it's  what makes us special so he thinks  that's what we're meant to be we're  designed to be dark we're designed to be  cruel and designed to be self-interested  so lean into that like I'll show you how  to be that you know win just win and so  that book is is deeply personal to me  and also extremely fun from a very very  sort of evil dark angle  I love the lettering I love the use of  that inside text text and I and I love  the perspective because it's a Batman  story that's never been told for those  very unique lens but we've also got  things like like the Batman himself I  wonder for you since you've written him  so much it's not a coincidence to me  that Heath Ledger's Joker was the first  mainstream Oscar and now with this black  label arc of the films we're looking at  a circuit basically run for the Joker  it's not at Comic Con because it's  theoretically going on a film circuit  what do you think makes Batman so  universally approachable that he's  breaking the barriers of these  comic-book norms oh I think I mean I  think at heart he's a really simple  folktale I mean Batman is enduring to me  because he says you take any trauma  anything bad in your life and you use it  as fuel to do good you know you make  your life matter our version of Batman  is very much about saying you win this  Batman wins when you recognize that  everything you do mean something  every day every moment do something that  makes the world better Joker is the  opposite to us Joker is the one that  says you win when you finally realize  that nothing you do matters and you just  embrace the insanity of thinking so and  the absurdity of that and Batman you  laugh is just like when he doesn't care  so that's why they make a really good  triangle but I think that's what makes  you know that for me that's what makes  him so adaptable and flexible and 80  years of seeing him everywhere from  space you know the deep-sea fighting  sharks to Gotham City yeah it's just  he's a really incredibly compelling  construction that just says  when something bad happens you take it  you use it as fuel to do something good  now all my shows Collider heroes and  comic-book shopping I talk about court  of owls so I would I would hate myself I  don't bring out that that masterpiece I  love that it falls in between a year one  and where we know the bat today and you  found the perfect way to kind of plant a  legacy and build a mythology within the  mythos without messing up continuity  where did that idea come from to go like  heater that's that's worked for me arc  after arc you know at least in terms of  my satisfaction with the work that we've  done not that it's perfect but just that  I've always tried to approach it as if  you only have one chance to write Batman  its this arc so what is the story you  need to tell right now and so each one  of the arcs that I did on Batman is  still to this day with metal and Batman  who laughs there about things that I  worry about for my kids so that I'm  hopeful about for myself or something on  my mind and court of owls really I wrote  it or thought of it at a time in my life  when I felt really displaced I was  really uncomfortable suddenly writing  Bruce Wayne at number one you know I  never thought I'd climb any ladder that  fast I never wanted to I was going back  to the city a lot to go into the office  to get help and in the office the DC  offices back when they were in New York  City were not far from where I grew up  so I would go from there back to the  neighborhood I grew up in and it was so  changed and nobody I knew lived there  anymore and there was this very humbling  sense of how the city you know or you  think you know changes right under your  feet and I knew that that was what I  wanted to do a story about Batman takes  all of his confidence from knowing  Gotham better than anybody else but what  if Gotham says you might know me right  now but you don't know me five years ago  ten years ago five years from now I'll  be a stranger to you 